Welcome to the KubeVirt on K3s playground!

Run virtual machines next to containers on a multi-node K3s cluster.

Machines:

  dev-machine  172.16.0.5  you are here: Docker, kubectl, virtctl, helm, k9s
  cplane-01    172.16.0.2  K3s server, KubeVirt control plane
  node-01      172.16.0.3  K3s agent, runs VMs
  node-02      172.16.0.4  K3s agent, runs VMs

KubeVirt and CDI are installed, and KubeVirt runs in emulation mode.
The KubeVirt Manager web UI is in the "KubeVirt Manager" tab (NodePort 30080).

Try it:

  kubectl get nodes
  kubectl -n kubevirt get kubevirt kubevirt
  kubectl apply -f ~/testvm.yaml
  virtctl start testvm
  kubectl get vmi -o wide     (see which node it landed on)
  virtctl console testvm      (user cirros, password gocubsgo, leave with Ctrl+])
  kubectl get cdi,storageclass

See a VM's screen in the browser:

  vm-vnc testvm               then open the VM Screen tab (Ctrl+C to stop)

Good to know:

- There is no nested virtualization here, so VMs use software emulation.
  Small images such as CirrOS boot in seconds, Ubuntu takes a few minutes.
- Every machine has zsh with a Tokyo Night prompt showing your Kubernetes
  context and namespace, plus eza, bat, fd, zoxide, atuin, lazygit and delta.
- Using a Nerd Font locally over `labctl ssh`? Run `icons on` for the
  powerline prompt and file icons. `icons off` switches back.
- KubeVirt Manager has no login. Anyone with the tab's link controls your VMs.
- Playground VMs are short-lived. Do not store anything important here.
